<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mendes</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mendes was an ancient Egyptian city in the north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile-delta">Nile Delta</a>, at present-day Tell el-Ruba. It was the cult centre of a ram god, the Ram of Mendes, and of the fish goddess Hatmehit. In the Third Intermediate Period it was ruled by Libyan chiefs, and around 399 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepherites-i">Nepherites I</a> of Mendes founded the Twenty-ninth Dynasty, which ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> from the city.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mendes.jpg" alt="Mendes" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Roland Unger · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:TellRubaLandscape.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Mendes is the Greek name of the ancient Egyptian city of Djedet, also called Per-Banebdjedet, the Domain of the Ram Lord of Djedet, and Anpet. It lay in the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile-delta">Nile Delta</a> on the Mendesian branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>, now silted up, about 35 kilometres east of al-Mansurah. The city was the capital of the 16th Lower Egyptian nome of Kha until it was replaced by Thmuis in Greco-Roman Egypt, and the two cities stood only several hundred metres apart. During the 29th Dynasty Mendes was also the capital of Egypt.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Archaeological evidence shows a town at Mendes at least as far back as the Naqada II period in the 4th millennium BCE. Under the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaohs</a> it became a strong seat of provincial government and remained so throughout the Egyptian period. Ancient geographers and historians, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a>, noticed it, and in Classical times it was known for a perfume called the Mendesium unguentum.</p><p>The city declined early and disappears in the first century CE, when Thmuis is the only town of note in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nome">nome</a>. Its position at the junction of the river and a lake probably exposed it to encroaching water after the canals fell into neglect under the Macedonian kings.</p><p>The chief deities were the ram Banebdjedet, the Ba of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osiris">Osiris</a>, and his consort, the fish goddess Hatmehit, who with their child Har-pa-khered, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horus">Horus</a> the Child, formed the triad of Mendes. Herodotus described the god as shown with the head and fleece of a goat. In 1855 the occultist Eliphas Levi drew the fictitious medieval idol Baphomet as a figure conflated with the goat of Mendes, an image later repeated by various occultists.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The site is the largest surviving tell in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile-delta">Nile Delta</a>, made up of Tell El-Ruba, the site of the main temple enclosure, and Tell El-Timai, the settlement site of Thmuis. It is about 3 kilometres long from north to south. An Old Kingdom necropolis is estimated to hold over 9,000 interments, and a cemetery of sacred rams was found in the northwest corner of Tell El-Ruba. Monuments bearing the names of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merneptah">Merneptah</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-iii">Ramesses III</a> were found, and a temple built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amasis-ii">Amasis II</a> is attested by its foundation deposits.</p><p>A joint team from the University of Washington and the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toronto">Toronto</a> discovered the tomb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepherites-i">Nepherites I</a> in 1992–1993, which Donald Redford concluded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> had destroyed. A sondage has revealed uninterrupted stratification from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-francia">Middle Kingdom</a> down to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-babylonian-empire">First Dynasty</a>, possibly the longest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mendes” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Psalms and Hymns for the Use of Jewish Children (1905), a closer one attributed to Frederick de Sola <b>Mendes</b> in The Jewish Encyclopedia (1906) and the Union Hymnal (1914), and two 21st-century versions that draw on these, in the Koren Sacks Siddur (2009) and the machzor Mishkan HaNefesh…</blockquote><a href="/songs/adon-olam">Adon Olam</a></li><li><blockquote>Chiefs of the Ma went on ruling Delta towns such as <b>Mendes</b> and Busiris until the Kushite Empire and later the Saite kings ended their independence by around 700 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/meshwesh">Meshwesh</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 399 to 398 BCE Amyrtaeus was overthrown by Nepherites I of <b>Mendes</b>, founder of the Twenty-ninth Dynasty of Egypt; an Aramaic papyrus suggests he was executed.</blockquote><a href="/state/twenty-eighth-dynasty-of-egypt">Twenty-eighth Dynasty of Egypt</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 399 to 398 BCE Nepherites I, a general from <b>Mendes</b> in the central Delta, overthrew Amyrtaeus of the Twenty-eighth Dynasty of Egypt and founded a new line.</blockquote><a href="/state/twenty-ninth-dynasty-of-egypt">Twenty-ninth Dynasty of Egypt</a></li></ul></section>
